<div class="wrap">
  <div class="cls">
    <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true" style="background:var(--cyan)"></span>
    <span>Field Account</span>
    <span>Sources Linked</span>
    <span class="sp"><b style="color:var(--cyan)">Record: open on purpose</b></span>
  </div>

  <header class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Notes from the Trenches // A calibration receipt, part two</p>
    <h1>The Drop I <em>Explained Too Early</em></h1>
    <p class="standfirst">USD Coin (USDC), Circle's dollar stablecoin, lost about $5.7B to $6.6B of supply in two weeks of January 2026. I had an explanation within days. It did not survive the first test, and what replaced it is still partly inference.</p>
    <p class="byline">Draft for review, not published &middot; Companion to the LinkedIn follow-up &middot; Every figure sits in a sibling evidence ledger</p>
    <p class="loopspine" aria-label="The loop: write the prior, run the test, correct in public"><b>write the prior</b> <i>&rarr;</i> <b>run the test</b> <i>&rarr;</i> <b>correct in public</b></p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <span class="num">I &middot; The sentence I wrote before the data</span>
    <p class="lead">On Feb 1 and Feb 2, 2026, two of my pieces said the January outflow was institutions dumping stablecoins after a Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) staff statement on Jan 28. It read like a clean story: a regulator speaks, a large holder sells, supply falls. I gave it a credence of 0.55 when I started testing it, the highest of any explanation on my list.</p>
    <p>The first test took one afternoon. I read the statement twice, with a word search. It names no Treasury bills, no liquid staking tokens and no wrappers. The explanation I had published had nothing to stand on, and its credence fell from 0.55 to 0.15 on that read alone. Once I measured the December baseline and the recovery, it fell to 0.05.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="num">II &middot; Fifteen days, two legs</span>
    <p>USDC, bitcoin and Solana all peaked on Jan 15 and 16. Supply turned with the crypto cycle at its high, not with a headline.</p>
    <p>The first leg ran from Jan 15 to Jan 22, about $3.1B down. Its sharpest day was Jan 20: Greenland tariff threats, record Japanese government bond yields, about $1.09B of liquidations (secondary reporting) and USDC down $1.9B, one of its two largest daily drops in the DefiLlama series. Hyperliquid's bitcoin funding rate flipped from about +11% to -8% annualized that day, the signature of crowded longs unwinding. The yen strengthened about 3% from Jan 20 to Jan 29. A cluster, not a cause: the evidence is timing.</p>
    <p>The second leg ran from Jan 22 to Jan 31. Binance announced a holding-rewards campaign for USD1, World Liberty Financial's stablecoin, on Jan 23: $40M of WLFI tokens to USD1 holders on Binance, Jan 23 to Feb 20 (secondary reporting). USD1 supply went from $3.17B to $4.93B in three days, $1.12B of it on Jan 23. From Jan 22 to Jan 31 USD1 gained $1.89B while USDC fell $3.55B (DefiLlama) or $4.39B (CoinGecko). That is about 43% to 53% of the leg. The Jan 30 liquidations (about $1.71B, secondary) and the Warsh nomination for Fed chair, also named Jan 30, fall in the same window. I scored the nomination as a liquidity shock (H34, 0.05 to 0.10): it hit risk assets more than the stablecoin float, USDC moved only -$0.24B on Jan 30 and +$0.07B on Jan 31, and the Jan 29 drop came before the announcement.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="num">III &middot; The ledger</span>
    <h2>Priors written down, then moved by tests</h2>

    <div class="panel">
      <h4>Belief log &middot; prior to final credence &middot; the rows that carry the story</h4>
      <p style="margin:0 0 14px">A credence is my subjective probability that a factor contributed more than about 10% of the move. Credences overlap and do not sum to one.</p>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="k">H1 &middot; Regulation-forced selling</span><b>0.55 to 0.05. Falsifier: the statement names the assets. <span style="color:var(--cyan)">PREMISE FAILED</span></b>
        <span class="k">H2 &middot; Deleveraging</span><b>0.20 to 0.50. Falsifier: drops with no liquidation event. <span style="color:var(--cyan)">BEST FIT, INFERENCE</span></b>
        <span class="k">H27 &middot; Binance USD1 campaign</span><b>0.05 to 0.40. Falsifier: USD1 flat on Jan 23 to 25. <span style="color:var(--cyan)">STRONG TIMING, MECHANISM UNSHOWN</span></b>
        <span class="k">H3 &middot; Macro risk-off</span><b>0.15 to 0.35. Falsifier: the drop precedes the shocks. <span style="color:var(--cyan)">PLAUSIBLE, OVERLAPS H2</span></b>
        <span class="k">H4 &middot; Tokenized Treasuries</span><b>0.10 to 0.20. About $0.8B moved, mostly one fund. <span style="color:var(--cyan)">REAL BUT SMALL</span></b>
        <span class="k">H7 &middot; Measurement</span><b>0.05 to 0.15. DefiLlama and CoinGecko differ by about $1B on some days. <span style="color:var(--cyan)">LIVE</span></b>
      </div>
      <p>The one explanation I had published started highest and ended lowest. The one I had barely considered, the exchange campaign, moved the most.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="num">IV &middot; Shape as evidence</span>
    <p>Peak to trough, January was -9.2% over 14 days, and USDC regained 95% of its prior peak 12 days after the low. March 2023, the Silicon Valley Bank episode, was -26.9% over 39 days with no recovery in the window. November 2025, the Stream collapse, was -2.9%. A fast V is how a demand-for-liquidity episode looks; a trust shock does not rebound that way. The shape favors the deleveraging and macro readings and argues against forced institutional selling.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="num">V &middot; The tests that came back empty</span>
    <p>Empty is a result. Each of these had a falsifier written in advance, and none found its effect: the SEC text (names no assets); the Oct 10 cascade (it hit USDe, and USDC rose afterward); a Solana rotation (Solana lost about $2.25B of USDC too); the Iran war (it began Feb 28, six weeks after the largest drops); sanctions (the Jan 30 designation targeted USDT on Tron, a day later); foreign selling of Treasuries (foreigners were net buyers, $63.5B); credit stress (spreads near year lows); custodian gating (no notice found); hacks (about $370M in January, mostly not USDC); consumer confidence (best month of the year); collateral repricing (DAI plus USDS flat at $10.9B while ether fell); tokenized gold (about $0.25B, with gold at a record); hedge fund shorts (cash and bill margined, USDC margin is locked and not burned); the Circle lockup expiry (equity proceeds are dollars); Gemini and Genesis fallout (the SEC dismissed its Gemini case on Jan 23, pointing the wrong way); reserve diversification (the dollar's share did not fall); and the weekly correlations, which never cleared a multiple-comparison bar across more than a dozen tests.</p>
    <p>One test was untestable, not empty: the European Union's DAC8 tax-reporting rules. There is no redemption data to run it against.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="num">VI &middot; Three corrections</span>
    <p>My Flippening piece set the falsifier at USDC returning to about $80B. The real December level was $75B to $77B, USDC closed 2025 at $75.3B, and it was back to $75.2B by Feb 28. My own falsifier fired, and my first draft of part one missed it by reading a September snapshot.</p>
    <p>The central premise of two pieces failed on a read of the primary text. That is row H1 above.</p>
    <p>And the headline number is a range because two sources disagree, and the daily points in one of them are 00:00 UTC snapshots, a day off from events. Part of the size of the move may be measurement.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="num">VII &middot; Pre-mortem: the Convex Skeptic</span>
    <div class="panel">
      <h4>Pre-mortem &middot; the Convex Skeptic vs. the record</h4>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="k">Skeptic says</span><b>You are fitting a story to a V-shape.</b>
        <span class="k">Record shows</span><b>Two legs fit the timing, and neither is stated as cause. The data that would decide it is not public.</b>
        <span class="k">Skeptic says</span><b>A rewards campaign is not a redemption.</b>
        <span class="k">Record shows</span><b>Correct. It is the one thing I could not show. The 43% to 53% is an overlap in size, not a funding trace.</b>
        <span class="k">Skeptic says</span><b>Your credences are made up.</b>
        <span class="k">Record shows</span><b>They are subjective, and the log says so. What can be checked is the order of the tests and the falsifiers written first.</b>
      </div>
      <p>Silence counts as evidence in this method only for required, routine disclosures. A 10-K that reports year-end figures says nothing about January by omission, so I treat its silence as no information.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Several stories about political and commercial conflicts of interest touch this period: a pardon tied to a Binance founder, a loan tied to a cabinet official and Tether, an adviser's holdings, a lawsuit against the Internal Revenue Service. They are alleged or disputed, and for each I found no flow link to USDC supply. They are not in the account above.</p>
    <blockquote>Silence is evidence only where someone was required to speak.</blockquote>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="num">VIII &middot; What would settle it</span>
    <p>The deciding question is whether the USD1 mints were funded by USDC redemptions. Circle does not publish who redeemed, and a coin in a cold wallet or a frozen vault is still in circulation; supply falls only when someone redeems with the issuer. Two things could answer it: a list of USDC burns by entity from a paid on-chain analytics vendor, and Binance conversion data for the campaign window. Until then the account stays at two legs and one open thread, which is where the record puts it.</p>
    <p>This is analysis, not investment advice.</p>
  </section>

